+### [3-D Integer Wavelet Packet Transform with Implicit Bit Shifting](8250-3-d-integer-wavelet-packet-transform-with-implicit-bit-shift.md)
+This method provides a structure for 3-D integer wavelet transforms that allows coefficients to be scaled using implicit bit shifts to approximate a unitary transformation. It solves the problem where standard integer wavelet transforms are not unitary, causing quantization error in the wavelet domain to differ from the Mean Squared Error (MSE) in the spatial domain, which compromises lossy coding performance. Unlike lifting-based scaling, this approach constrains scaling factors to powers of two, enabling efficient implementation via bit shifts and improving lossless coding efficiency by forcing certain bit planes of large coefficients to zero. It is specifically designed to address the difficulty in 3-D dyadic transforms where scaling factors in the third dimension are not naturally powers of two.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Higher than DWT due to the decomposition of all subbands. The number of nodes in a full tree is $2^{L+1}-1$ for depth $L$.
-- **Memory Complexity:** Higher than DWT. The full tree stores all subbands.
+- **Time Complexity:** Higher than standard DWT due to the decomposition of all subbands and the need for tree optimization [S1, S2].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Higher than DWT due to the larger number of coefficient nodes [S1, S2].
 - **Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Strengths:** Richer decomposition allows for better adaptation to signal characteristics.
-- **Limitations:** High computational cost and memory usage. Not typically used in volume rendering [S1][S2].
-- **When to use:** Volume segmentation [S1][S2].
-- **When not to use:** Volume rendering [S1][S2].
+- **Strengths:** Richer decomposition allows for better adaptation to local data features; optimal basis selection can improve compression ratios for specific data types [S1, S2].
+- **Limitations:** High computational cost for tree construction and optimization; not typically used in volume rendering [S1, S2].
+- **When to use:** Volume segmentation tasks where optimal basis selection is critical [S1, S2].
+- **When not to use:** Real-time volume rendering where computational efficiency is paramount [S1, S2].
 
 ## Background
-The Discrete Wavelet Transform (DWT) was introduced for compressed volume rendering by Muraki [Mur93] [S1][S2]. The DWPT offers an even richer decomposition [S1][S2]. The method has found application in volume segmentation [ASA11] [S1][S2], but is not typically applied in volume rendering [S1][S2]. The work of Westermann et al. [Wes94] indicates that the wavelet transform is particularly suited for multiresolution volume visualization [S1][S2].
+The Discrete Wavelet Transform (DWT) was introduced for compressed volume rendering by Muraki [Mur93], triggering many follow-up works [Wes94, GEA96, GLDH97, LGK97, IP99, Rod99, KS99, NS01, GWGS02, LLYM04, WSKW05, WQ05, She06, KLW*08, WM08] [S1, S2]. The DWPT offers an even richer decomposition than DWT [S1, S2]. The work of Westermann et al. [Wes94] indicates that the wavelet transform is particularly suited for multiresolution volume visualization. Guthe et al. [GWGS02] created a block-wise hierarchical decomposition (octree) of volume data followed by entropy encoding of wavelet coefficients, introducing priority-based decompression using block caching and projective classification during rendering [S1, S2]. This approach is currently shared by many compressed rendering systems [S1, S2]. However, the DWPT itself has found application primarily in volume segmentation [ASA11] and is not typically applied in volume rendering [S1, S2].
 
